D. Tournament Analysis setup and proof use
  vertices: speeds inside a fixed row; alternate vertices considered were
    denominator shells, unit numerators, dilated bands, deleted-core addresses,
    cover obligations, owner/Bprime exits, and proof tactics.
  selected quotient: speed dominance over pre-height dilated-band cover.
  pairwise observable: sum_q |U_v(q)\U_w(q)| - |U_w(q)\U_v(q)| for q<h(S).
  switch/gauge: q starts at the band-1 threshold 14; q<h means the row blocks q.
  tie Hamiltonian path: row order.
  preserves: who carries the blocked-unit cover before the first witness.
  destroys: continuous-time owner geometry and Bprime interval widths.
  proof interpretation: high blocking height is a long perfect-cover streak;
    if raw dominance grows but normalized dominance thins, the proof target
    becomes a dichotomy between peelable carriers and balanced-cover rigidity.

E. Takeaway
  Dominance grows with blocking height only in the raw cumulative sense:
  mean pair margins rise strongly because a high-h row blocks many shells.
  Height-normalized dominance moves the other way, and the binary tournament
  saturates to a transitive order in these samples (score spread 12, no
  directed 3-cycles).  The hard rows therefore look less like arbitrary
  hierarchies and more like long balanced-cover chains with accumulated
  but diluted dominance.
  Proof route: show that every long blocking-height row either has a
  peelable carrier whose cumulative excess forces a witness after deletion,
  or else satisfies balanced-cover congruences tight enough to enter the
  ramified Q31/band-2 portal already isolated by HYP-2471/HYP-2480.
